* The prime factors of 13911, worked out one subtraction at a time.
*
* 13911 does not fit in a cell. A cell holds 0 to 255, so the number, the
* quotient and every intermediate value here are pairs of cells -- `hi` and
* `lo`, worth hi*256 + lo -- and `lib/wide.bfm` is the arithmetic on them.
* That library is the point of this program as much as the answer is: working
* in numbers wider than a cell was written down as something the *language*
* would have to grow, and it turns out to be a library, because a parameter
* can name a cell.
*
* The algorithm is trial division, the way it is done by hand. Take the number
* down to zero one at a time, cycling a counter that resets every `v` steps;
* when it lands exactly on zero, `v` divides the number, and the number of
* resets is the quotient. It is the slowest possible way to divide and the
* only one that needs no comparison, which in a language without a conditional
* is the trade worth making. Roughly 340,000 decrements answer the whole
* question.
*
* Stopping is the part that needs care. A factor above the square root of a
* number cannot exist without a matching one below it, so once the quotient is
* smaller than the divisor, whatever is left is prime. That is one comparison,
* and it saves trying every divisor up to 255.
*
* It prints `13911: 3 4637`, which is what
* `programs/third-party/advanced/factor.bf` prints when given 13911 -- a
* program somebody else wrote, and the reason the answer here is checked
* against something other than itself.

@include "lib/wide.bfm"

* Declaration order is a performance decision, and this is the one place in
* the corpus where it shows. The expander gives cells out in the order they are
* declared, and `@to` emits the movement between them -- so the cells the
* division touches on every step are declared together, at the front, and the
* ones it touches once a factor are behind them. Nothing about the program's
* meaning depends on this; how far the cursor walks does.

* The working register: every division happens here, and the quotient is left
* in it. Where a quotient is built up while the register is emptied.
@var x_hi
@var x_lo
@var t_hi
@var t_lo

@var count        * how much of a subtraction is left to do
@var v            * the divisor being tried
@var flag
@var other
@var held
@var scratch
@var spare
@var going
@var zero
@var was_low      * whether the high half was empty before a subtraction
@var wrapped      * whether it holds 255 after one
@var r            * what was left over

* The number being factored, and what is left of it as factors come out.
@var n_hi
@var n_lo

@var ten          * the constant, for turning a number into digits

@var d0           * the digits of a number being printed, least significant
@var d1
@var d2
@var d3
@var d4
@var started      * whether a non-zero digit has been printed yet

@var running      * whether there is any factoring left to do
@var smaller      * whether the quotient came out below the divisor
@var passed       * whether one cell ran out before the other
@var step         * a cell counted down against another

@var out


* Whether a cell holds 255. Adding one and asking whether the answer came out
* zero is cheaper than comparing against 255, and it is the only comparison
* BrainFuck gives away free.
*
* A high half of 255 after a subtraction is what going below zero looks like --
* but only if the high half was empty before it, since a number in the top
* 256th of the range starts there. Both halves of that are checked below;
* checking only the second one factors every number correctly except the
* largest 256, which is the kind of wrong that ships.
@macro holds_255(cell, flag) {
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(cell, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    +
    @put(flag, 1)
    @to scratch
    [
        @clear(flag)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
}

* The working register divided by whatever `divisor` names: the quotient is
* left in the working register and the remainder in `r`.
*
* Long division without the division: take the divisor off, and off again, and
* count how many times it went. When one subtraction too many takes the number
* below zero, put it back -- what is left is the remainder.
*
* Taking `v` off at once rather than counting down to it one step at a time is
* the difference between this finishing in a second and finishing in two
* minutes. BrainFuck cannot ask whether a cell is zero without spending its
* value to find out, so a test inside the innermost loop is never cheap; this
* way there is one test per multiple of the divisor rather than one per step.
*
* One routine, used for both halves of the program -- trial division tries it
* with the divisor, and printing a number tries it with ten.
@macro divide(divisor) {
    @wide_clear(t_hi, t_lo)
    @put(going, 1)
    @to going
    [
        * Whether there is anything above the low half to borrow from, asked
        * before the subtraction because afterwards is too late to tell.
        @is_zero(x_hi, was_low, scratch, spare)

        @clear(count)
        @copy(divisor, count, spare)
        @to count
        [
            @wide_dec(x_hi, x_lo, flag, held)
            @to count
            -
        ]

        @holds_255(x_hi, wrapped)
        @to was_low
        [
            @to wrapped
            [
                * One too many. Put it back, and the loop is done.
                @clear(count)
                @copy(divisor, count, spare)
                @to count
                [
                    @wide_inc(x_hi, x_lo, flag, held)
                    @to count
                    -
                ]
                @clear(going)
                @clear(wrapped)
            ]
            @clear(was_low)
        ]
        @clear(wrapped)

        * It went: that is one more multiple.
        @copy(going, other, spare)
        @to other
        [
            @wide_inc(t_hi, t_lo, flag, held)
            @clear(other)
        ]
        @to going
    ]

    * What is left is smaller than the divisor, so it is in the low half.
    @clear(r)
    @move(x_lo, r)
    @clear(x_hi)
    @wide_copy(t_hi, t_lo, x_hi, x_lo, scratch)
}

* A digit, unless it is a leading zero.
@macro show_maybe(digit) {
    @is_not_zero(digit, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @put(started, 1)
        @clear(flag)
    ]
    @copy(started, flag, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @show_digit(digit)
        @clear(flag)
    ]
}

* The working register, in decimal, most significant digit first. Dividing by
* ten five times peels the digits off backwards, so they are kept and then
* read in the other order. Five is enough: two cells cannot hold 100,000.
@macro show_working {
    @clear(d0)
    @clear(d1)
    @clear(d2)
    @clear(d3)
    @clear(d4)
    @divide(ten)
    @move(r, d0)
    @divide(ten)
    @move(r, d1)
    @divide(ten)
    @move(r, d2)
    @divide(ten)
    @move(r, d3)
    @divide(ten)
    @move(r, d4)

    @clear(started)
    @show_maybe(d4)
    @show_maybe(d3)
    @show_maybe(d2)
    @show_maybe(d1)
    @put(started, 1)
    @show_maybe(d0)
}

* A single cell, in decimal. The divisor never reaches 256, so it goes in the
* low half of the working register and out through the same digits.
@macro show_byte(cell) {
    @wide_clear(x_hi, x_lo)
    @copy(cell, x_lo, spare)
    @show_working
}

* The number, in decimal, without disturbing it.
@macro show_number {
    @wide_copy(n_hi, n_lo, x_hi, x_lo, scratch)
    @show_working
}

* Whether the quotient in the working register came out below the divisor.
*
* Anything with a high half is at least 256, and no divisor here reaches that,
* so only the low halves are ever compared. The comparison itself is `@less`
* from the idiom library -- it was written out here first, which is how it
* ended up in the library.
@macro quotient_is_smaller {
    @clear(smaller)
    @is_zero(x_hi, zero, scratch, spare)
    @to zero
    [
        @less(x_lo, v, smaller, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare)
        @clear(zero)
    ]
}


* 13911 is 54 * 256 + 87.
@wide_put(n_hi, n_lo, 54, 87)
@put(ten, 10)

@show_number
@say(out, ':')

@put(v, 2)
@put(running, 1)
@to running
[
    @wide_copy(n_hi, n_lo, x_hi, x_lo, scratch)
    @divide(v)

    * Nothing left over: the divisor is a factor, and the quotient is what is
    * left to factor. The same divisor is tried again, since it may go in more
    * than once.
    @is_zero(r, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @wide_copy(x_hi, x_lo, n_hi, n_lo, scratch)
        @say(out, SPACE)
        @show_byte(v)
        @clear(flag)
    ]

    * Something left over: either the divisor has gone past the square root,
    * in which case what is left is prime and the work is done, or it is time
    * to try the next one.
    @is_not_zero(r, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @quotient_is_smaller
        @is_zero(smaller, other, scratch, spare)
        @to other
        [
            @to v
            +
            @clear(other)
        ]

        * A divisor is one cell, so the next one after 255 is zero -- and
        * dividing by zero takes nothing away for ever. Any number this
        * program can hold is either prime by now or has a factor below 256,
        * since two factors above 255 multiply past 65535, so stopping here
        * is not giving up. The wrap is read off the cell rather than
        * compared against 255, which is the only comparison free here.
        @is_zero(v, other, scratch, spare)
        @to other
        [
            @clear(running)
            @clear(other)
        ]
        @to smaller
        [
            @clear(running)
            @clear(smaller)
        ]
        @clear(flag)
    ]

    * A number that has come all the way down to one has nothing left in it.
    @wide_copy(n_hi, n_lo, x_hi, x_lo, scratch)
    @wide_dec(x_hi, x_lo, flag, held)
    @wide_is_zero(x_hi, x_lo, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @clear(running)
        @clear(flag)
    ]

    @to running
]

* Whatever is left, if it is not one, is the last prime factor.
@wide_copy(n_hi, n_lo, x_hi, x_lo, scratch)
@wide_dec(x_hi, x_lo, flag, held)
@wide_is_not_zero(x_hi, x_lo, flag, scratch, spare)
@to flag
[
    @say(out, SPACE)
    @show_number
    @clear(flag)
]
@newline(out)
